class pair {
    int r;
    int c;

    public pair(int r, int c) {
        this.r = r;
        this.c = c;
    }
}

class Solution {
    public static void bfs(int r, int c, boolean[][] vis, char[][] grid) {
        Queue<pair> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.add(new pair(r, c));
        while (q.size() > 0) {
            pair front = q.remove();
            int row = front.r;
            int col = front.c;

            //top
            if (row > 0) {
                if (vis[row - 1][col] == false && grid[row - 1][col] == '1') {
                    q.add(new pair(row - 1, col));
                    vis[row - 1][col] = true;
                }
            }
            //bootom
            if (row + 1 < grid.length) {
                if (vis[row + 1][col] == false && grid[row + 1][col] == '1') {
                    q.add(new pair(row + 1, col));
                    vis[row + 1][col] = true;
                }
            }
            //left 
            if (col > 0) {
                if (vis[row][col - 1] == false && grid[row][col - 1] == '1') {
                    q.add(new pair(row, col - 1));
                    vis[row][col - 1] = true;
                }
            }
            //right 
            if (col + 1 < grid[0].length) {
                if (vis[row][col + 1] == false && grid[row][col + 1] == '1') {
                    q.add(new pair(row, col + 1));
                    vis[row][col + 1] = true;
                }
            }

        }

    }

    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {
        int row = grid.length;
        int col = grid[0].length;
        boolean[][] vis = new boolean[row][col];
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < grid.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < grid[0].length; j++) {
                if (vis[i][j] == false && grid[i][j] == '1') {
                    bfs(i, j, vis, grid);
                    count++;
                }

            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}


// Approach 

intuition

The grid contains two types of cells:

'1' represents land.

'0' represents water.

An island is a group of connected land cells connected horizontally or vertically.

The key observation is that all connected land cells belong to the same island. Therefore, whenever we encounter an unvisited land cell, we have discovered a new island.

We use BFS to explore all land cells connected to that starting cell and mark them as visited. This ensures that the same island is not counted multiple times.

Approach

Create a visited matrix of the same dimensions as the grid to keep track of visited cells.

Traverse the entire grid using two nested loops.

Whenever we find an unvisited land cell ('1'):

Increment the island count.

Start BFS from that cell using a queue.

During BFS:

Remove a cell from the queue.

Check its four adjacent cells: top, bottom, left, and right.

If an adjacent cell is within the grid boundaries, contains land ('1'), and has not been visited, mark it as visited and add it to the queue.

Continue until the queue becomes empty, meaning all land cells belonging to that island have been explored.

Return the total island count after traversing the grid.

Complexity

Time complexity:
O(m × n)

Where m is the number of rows and n is the number of columns.

We traverse every cell in the grid, and each land cell is added to the BFS queue at most once. Therefore, the overall time complexity is O(m × n).

Space complexity:
O(m × n)

The visited matrix requires O(m × n) space. In the worst case, the BFS queue can also contain O(m × n) cells.

Hence, the overall auxiliary space complexity is O(m × n).

